1. Data Architecture

Data Collection Layer
Web Server Logs: Capture user interactions on the website (pages viewed, add-to-cart, purchase events).
Sales Transactions: Historical and real-time transactional data from the POS systems.
Customer Data: Demographic and behavioral data from CRM systems.
Product Data: Catalog data including SKU, category, specifications, and inventory levels.
Third-party Data: Integrate external data like market trends, competitor pricing, or weather data if relevant.

Data Processing Layer
Raw Data Storage: Use a staging area on high-capacity NAS (Network Attached Storage) for raw, incoming data.
Data Transformation: Implement ETL processes to clean, standardize, and enrich data.
Data Validation: Ensure data quality with checks for consistency, accuracy, and completeness.

Data Storage Layer
Data Warehouse: Centralized SQL database for analytical reporting, built on high-performance, scalable on-premises servers.

Data Access Layer
Business Intelligence Tools: Connect BI tools (e.g., Tableau, Power BI) to the data warehouse for ad-hoc querying and visualization.
API Layer: Develop RESTful APIs for secure data access by internal applications.

